\layout Standard

Le probleme physique etudie celui de la generation de courant dans un plasma
 resultant d'une faible distortion de la distribution des electrons, il
 est d'usage de lineariser l'operateur de collisions autour du noyau Maxwellien.
 Un discussion detaillee de cette question est abordee dans l'article de
 revue de C.
 Karney sur les differentes formes possibles que l'on peut utiliser pour
 l'operateur de collision dans les calculs numeriques.
 On considerera ici trois formes, l'operateur Maxwellien 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
C_{M}(f^{e})=C_{ee}(f^{e},f_{M}^{e})+C_{ei}(f^{e},f_{M}^{i})
\]
\end_inset 

 decrivant les collisions entre les electrons rapides et les electrons ainsi
 que les ions dominants dont la distribution en impulsion est de type Maxwellien
,
 caracterises respectivement par les distribution 
\begin_inset Formula \( f_{M}^{e}\)
\end_inset 

 et 
\begin_inset Formula \( f_{M}^{i},\)
\end_inset 

 la distribution deformee par les differentes interactions etant ici denommee
 par 
\begin_inset Formula \( f^{e}.\)
\end_inset 

 Pour le probleme de la generation de courant il est important de considerer
 le terme de correction d'ordre un de l'operateur de collision des electrons
 thermiques sur la queue non-thermique.
 Dans ce cas, on parle de l'operateur de collision linearise (ou tronque)
 qui est la somme de trois termes 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
C_{M}(f^{e})=C_{ee}(f^{e},f_{M}^{e})+C_{ee}(f_{M}^{e},f^{e})+C_{ei}(f^{e},f_{M}^{i})
\]
\end_inset 

 C'est l'operateur de collision generalement employe pour les simulations
 plasmas.
 A noter qu'il introduit une legere non-linearite dans l'equation de diffusion,
 ce qui necessite d'effectuer plusieurs iterations meme en presence d'un
 schema numerique totalement implicite.
 Enfin, il est interessant de considerer pour des comparaisons analytiques
 la limite haute vitesse de l'operateur de collision 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
C_{M}^{HV}(f^{e})=C_{ee}^{HV}(f^{e},f_{M}^{e})+C_{ee}^{HV}(f_{M}^{e},f^{e})+C_{ei}^{HV}(f^{e},f_{M}^{i})
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Terme differentiel 
\begin_inset Formula \( C_{ee}(f,f_{M})+C_{ei}(f,f_{i})\)
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

L'operateur de collision decrivant les interactions binaires entre electrons
 et ions est exprime sous la forme conservative 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
C_{M}(f^{e})=C_{ee}(f^{e},f_{M}^{e})+C_{ei}(f^{e},f_{M}^{i})=\frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial p}\left[ p^{2}\left( A(p)\frac{\partial f^{e}}{\partial p}+F(p)f^{e}\right) \right] +\frac{B_{t}(p)}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial \xi }\left[ (1-\xi ^{2})\frac{\partial f^{e}}{\partial \xi }\right] 
\]
\end_inset 

 ou le terme 
\begin_inset Formula \( F(p)\)
\end_inset 

 represente le ralentissement collisionnel 
\begin_inset Formula \( 
F(p)=F^{(e)}(p)+F^{(i)}(p)\)
\end_inset 

 qui est un terme de force (convection).
 Normalise a la quantite 
\begin_inset Formula \( \nu _{e}p_{Te},\)
\end_inset 

 le terme de collision avec les electrons vaut 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
F^{(e)}(p)=F_{1}+F_{2}
\]
\end_inset 

 ou 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
F_{1} &=&4\pi \int_{0}^{p}f_{M}(p^{\prime })\frac{p^{\prime }{}^{2}}{\gamma
^{\prime }}\left[ \frac{1}{v^{2}}+\frac{1}{p^{2}}\left( 1-\frac{\gamma
^{\prime }\sigma ^{\prime }}{z^{\prime }}\right) \right] dp^{\prime } \\
F_{2} &=&\frac{4\pi }{v}\left( 1-\frac{\sigma }{\gamma z}\right) \int_{p}^{\infty }p^{\prime }f_{M}(p^{\prime })dp^{\prime }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 la quantite de mouvement 
\begin_inset Formula \( p\)
\end_inset 

 etant normalisee a l'impulsion thermique 
\begin_inset Formula \( p_{Te}
\)
\end_inset 

, avec les definitions suivantes 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
z=p\frac{p_{Te}}{m_{e}c}\qquad \sigma =\sinh ^{-1}z\qquad v=\frac{p}{\gamma }
\qquad \gamma =\sqrt{1+z^{2}}
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

La contribution ionique est donnee simplement par 
\begin_inset Formula \( F^{(i)}(p)=\frac{1}{v^{2}}
Z_{i}\frac{m_{e}}{m_{i}}\)
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Diffusion in Momentum
\layout Standard


\series bold 

\latex latex 

\backslash  
\latex default 

\begin_inset Formula \( p\)
\end_inset 


\series default 
, described by the diffusion coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula \( 
A(p)=A^{(e)}(p)+A^{(i)}(p)\)
\end_inset 

, which also includes a contribution from collisions with electrons and
 with ions. 
\begin_inset Formula \( A(p)\)
\end_inset 

 is normalized to the thermal diffusion coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula \( \nu _{e}p_{Te}^{2}\)
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

Collisions with electrons 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
A^{(e)}(p)=\frac{F_{1}+F_{2}}{v}
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Collisions with ions 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
A^{(i)}(p)=\frac{1}{v^{3}}Z_{i}\frac{m_{e}}{m_{i}}\frac{T_{i}}{T_{e}}
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Pitch-angle Scattering (Diffusion in 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \)
\end_inset 

)
\layout Standard

, described by the diffusion coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{t}(p)=B_{t}^{(e)}(p)+B_{t}^{(i)}(p)\)
\end_inset 

, normalized to 
\begin_inset Formula \( \nu _{e}p_{Te}^{2}\)
\end_inset 

 with
\layout Standard

Collisions with electrons 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{t}^{(e)}(p)=B_{1}+B_{2}
\]
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
B_{1} &=&4\pi \int_{0}^{p}f_{M}(p^{\prime })\frac{p^{\prime }{}^{2}}{v}
\left\{ \frac{1}{2}-\frac{p^{\prime }{}^{2}}{6p^{2}}+\frac{1}{8\gamma
^{\prime }\gamma ^{2}z^{2}}\left[ -3\gamma ^{\prime }+\sigma ^{\prime
}\left( \frac{3}{z^{\prime }}+2z^{\prime }\right) \right] \cdots \right.  \\
&&\,\,\left. -\frac{1}{4z^{2}\gamma ^{\prime }}\left( \gamma ^{\prime }-\frac{\sigma ^{\prime }}{z^{\prime }}-\frac{2}{3}\gamma ^{\prime }z^{\prime
}{}^{2}\right) -\frac{1}{4\gamma ^{\prime }\gamma ^{2}}\left( \gamma
^{\prime }-\frac{\sigma ^{\prime }}{z^{\prime }}\right) \right\} dp^{\prime }
\\
B_{2} &=&4\pi \int_{p}^{\infty }p^{\prime }f_{M}(p^{\prime })\frac{p^{\prime
}{}^{2}}{v^{\prime }}\left\{ \frac{1}{2}-\frac{\gamma ^{2}}{6\gamma ^{\prime
}{}^{2}}+\frac{1}{8\gamma \gamma ^{\prime }{}^{2}z^{2}}\left[ -3\gamma
+\sigma \left( \frac{3}{z}+2z\right) \right] \cdots \right.  \\
&&\,\,\left. -\frac{\gamma }{4z^{2}\gamma ^{\prime }{}^{2}}\left( \gamma -\frac{\sigma }{z}-\frac{2}{3}\gamma z^{2}\right) -\frac{v^{\prime }{}^{2}}{
4\gamma p^{2}}\left( \gamma -\frac{\sigma }{z}\right) \right\} dp^{\prime }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Collisions with ions 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{t}^{(i)}(p)=\frac{1}{2v}Z_{i}\left( 1-\frac{1}{v^{2}}\frac{m_{e}}{m_{i}}\frac{T_{i}}{T_{e}}\right) 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

It is interesting to note that collisions with ions bring only a very small
 contribution (ratio 
\begin_inset Formula \( m_{e}/m_{i}\)
\end_inset 

) to the slowing down and diffusion in 
\begin_inset Formula \( p\)
\end_inset 

 processes.
 However, the contribution of ions for pitch-angle scattering is of the
 same order as the contribution from electrons.
 Expression in the Limit of a Lorentz Gaz In the limit of a Lorentz gas
 (
\begin_inset Formula \( Z_{i}\gg 1\)
\end_inset 

), pitch-angle scattering over ions becomes the dominant collision process
 and the collision operator then reduces from () to: 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
C^{(\text{Lor.})}(f)=\frac{\nu _{e}Z}{2v}\frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial \xi }\left[ (1-\xi ^{2})\frac{\partial f}{\partial \xi }\right] =\frac{\nu _{ei}}{2\gamma ^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial \xi }\left[ (1-\xi
^{2})\frac{\partial f}{\partial \xi }\right] 
\]
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\nu _{ei}=\frac{e^{4}Zi^{2}n_{i}\ln \Lambda }{4\pi \epsilon
_{0}^{2}m_{e}^{2}v^{3}}
\]
\end_inset 

 Expression as the Divergence of a Flux and Bounce-Averaged Operator The
 differential part of the collision operator can also be expressed as the
 divergence of a flux: 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
C_{ee}(f,f_{M})+C_{ei}(f,f_{i})=-\nabla \cdot \vec{S}^{C}
\]
\end_inset 

 where the expression of the divergence operator in spherical coordinates
 is 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\nabla \cdot \vec{S}=\frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial p}(p^{2}S_{p})-\frac{1}{p}\frac{\partial }{\partial \xi }(\sqrt{1-\xi ^{2}}S_{\xi })
\]
\end_inset 

 Therefore, the expressions for the collisions-related fluxes are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
S_{p}^{C} &=&-\left[ A(p)\frac{\partial f}{\partial p}+F(p)f\right]  \\
S_{\xi }^{C} &=&\frac{B_{t}(p)}{p}\sqrt{1-\xi ^{2}}\frac{\partial f}{\partial \xi }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 A bounce-averaging of the collision operator is required in order to remove
 the dependence upon the poloidal angle 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 

 and reduce the Fokker-Planck equation to a 2-D momentum space equation.
 This operation is described in (), and has been derived for the collision
 operator in , for the case of 
\begin_inset Formula \( f_{0}\)
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \( g\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \tilde{f}\)
\end_inset 

, which gives:
\layout Standard

For 
\begin_inset Formula \( f_{0}\)
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Formula \( g\)
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\{\nabla \cdot \vec{S}^{C}\}_{f_{0},g}=\frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial p}(p^{2}S_{p}^{C,(0)})-\frac{1}{\lambda p}\frac{\partial }{\partial 
\xi _{0}}(\sqrt{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}\lambda S_{\xi _{0}}^{C,(0)})
\]
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
S_{p}^{C,(0)} &=&-\left[ A(p)\frac{\partial f^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})}{\partial p}
+F(p)f^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})\right]  \\
S_{\xi _{0}}^{C,(0)} &=&\frac{B_{t}(p)}{p}\sqrt{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}\left( 1-\frac{\Delta }{\xi _{0}^{2}}\right) \frac{\partial f^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})}{\partial \xi _{0}}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For 
\begin_inset Formula \( \tilde{f}\)
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\{\nabla \cdot \vec{S}^{C}\}_{\tilde{f}}=\frac{1}{p^{2}}\frac{\partial }{\partial p}(p^{2}\tilde{S}_{p}^{C,(0)})-\frac{1}{\lambda p}\frac{\partial }{\partial \xi _{0}}(\sqrt{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}\lambda \tilde{S}_{\xi _{0}}^{C,(0)})
\]
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\tilde{S}_{p}^{C,(0)} &=&-\frac{1}{\lambda (1+\epsilon )}H(|\xi _{0}|>\xi
_{0T})\left[ A(p)\frac{\partial \tilde{f}^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})}{\partial p}+F(p)\tilde{f}^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})\right]  \\
\tilde{S}_{\xi _{0}}^{C,(0)} &=&\frac{1}{\lambda (1+\epsilon )}H(|\xi
_{0}|>\xi _{0T})\frac{B_{t}(p)}{p}\sqrt{1-\xi _{0}^{2}} \\
&&\qquad \left( \frac{\partial \tilde{f}^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})}{\partial \xi _{0}}-\frac{(\epsilon -\epsilon ^{2}/2)}{(1+\epsilon )\xi _{0}}\frac{\partial }{\partial \xi _{0}}\frac{\tilde{f}^{(0)}(p,\xi _{0})}{\xi _{0}}\right) 
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For 
\begin_inset Formula \( f_{0}\)
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Formula \( g\)
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\{C_{ee}(f_{M},f)\}_{f_{0},g}=\{\xi I_{1}(f_{M},f_{1})\}_{f_{0},g}=\frac{
s^{*}}{\lambda }\xi _{0}I_{1}(f_{M},f_{1}^{(0)})
\]
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
f_{1}^{(0)}=\frac{3}{2}\int_{-1}^{1}\xi _{0}f^{(0)}d\xi _{0}
\]
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \( s^{*}\)
\end_inset 

 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
s^{*}=H(|\xi _{0}|>\xi _{0T})\,\,\int_{-\pi }^{\pi }\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }
\Psi 
\]
\end_inset 

 This bounce-averaging coefficient is described in Appendix .
\layout Standard

For 
\begin_inset Formula \( \tilde{f}\)
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\{C_{ee}(f_{M},f)\}_{\tilde{f}}=\{\xi I_{1}(f_{M},f_{1})\}_{\tilde{f}}=\frac{
1}{\lambda }H(|\xi _{0}|>\xi _{0T})\xi _{0}I_{1}(f_{M},\tilde{f}_{1}^{(0)})
\]
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\tilde{f}_{1}^{(0)}=\frac{3}{2}\int_{-1}^{1}H(|\xi _{0}|>\xi _{0T})\,\,\xi
_{0}\tilde{s}^{*}\tilde{f}^{(0)}d\xi _{0}
\]
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \( \tilde{s}^{*}\)
\end_inset 

 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\tilde{s}^{*}=\int_{-\theta _{c}}^{\theta _{c}}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{
\xi }{\xi _{0}}
\]
\end_inset 

 and is described in Appendix. 
